unt</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-petg-parts/">PETG</a></span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure class="part-card"><img src="/assets/build-fixed-wing/build-008.jpg" alt="3D render of the motor mount nut: a large hexagonal nut." loading="lazy"><figcaption><strong>Motor mount nut</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-petg-parts/">PETG</a></span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure class="part-card"><img src="/assets/build-fixed-wing/build-009.jpg" alt="3D render of the rear wing band clip: a long, thin U-shaped channel that slips over the wing&#x27;s trailing edge." loading="lazy"><figcaption><strong>Wing band clip rear</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-petg-parts/">PETG</a></span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure class="part-card"><img src="/assets/build-fixed-wing/build-010.jpg" alt="3D render of a control horn: a slotted base with a rounded upright arm and one pushrod hole." loading="lazy"><figcaption><strong>Control Horn</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-petg-parts/">PETG</a></span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure class="part-card"><img src="/assets/build-fixed-wing/build-011.jpg" alt="3D render of the V-tail empennage clip: a square tube sleeve with two angled slots for the ruddervators." loading="lazy"><figcaption><strong>Empennage Clip V-Tail</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-petg-parts/">PETG</a></span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure class="part-card"><img src="/assets/build-fixed-wing/build-012.jpg" alt="3D render of the front wing band clip: a wide, flat, folded clip that slips over the wing&#x27;s leading edge." loading="lazy"><figcaption><strong>Wing band Clip Front</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-petg-parts/">PETG</a></span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure class="part-card"><img src="/assets/build-fixed-wing/build-013.jpg" alt="3D render of the V-tail servo bracket: two deep servo boxes on either side of a square tube sleeve." loading="lazy"><figcaption><strong>Servo Bracket V-Tail Option</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-tpu-parts/">64D TPU</a></span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure class="part-card"><img src="/assets/build-fixed-wing/build-014.jpg" alt="3D render of the optional landing gear: a ring that fits around the fuselage with two angled legs ending in axle holes." loading="lazy"><figcaption><strong>Landing Gear</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-tpu-parts/">64D TPU</a></span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure class="part-card"><img src="/assets/build-fixed-wing/build-015.jpg" alt="3D render of the 2 mm plywood empennage clip: a square tube sleeve with thin slots for plywood tail surfaces." loading="lazy"><figcaption><strong>2mm Plywood Empennage Clip</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-tpu-parts/">64D TPU</a></span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure class="part-card"><img src="/assets/build-fixed-wing/build-016.jpg" alt="3D render of the 2 mm plywood empennage clip with a tail skid extending downward." loading="lazy"><figcaption><strong>2mm Plywood Empennage Clip with tail skid</strong><span><a href="/resources/trainer-tpu-parts/">64D TPU</a></span></figcaption></figure></li></ul>
<h3 id="other-materials-required">Other materials required</h3>
<ul><li>1 - 300x300x2mm (12”x12”x3/32”) Basswood plywood sheet</li><li>1 - M3 x 55mm hex bolt (Fuselage to carbon fiber rod)</li><li>3 - M3 x 20mm hex bolt (Empennage clip, Servo mount, Wing support)</li><li>4 - M3 nuts (Empennage clip, Servo mount, Wing support, fuselage to carbon fiber rod)</li><li>2 - M5 x 14mm hex bolts (3 if using landing gear)</li><li>2- M5 x 30 hex bolts (Wing rubberband supports)</li><li>1 - Tube of CA (Superglue)</li></ul>
<p class="small">(Flitetest Power Pack C or similar will include all electronics except Tx and Rx Batteries and charger)</p>
<ul><li>1 - 2218 1180KV Brushless motor</li><li>4 - 9 gram metal gear servos</li><li>1 - 9x4.7 slowfly propeller</li><li>1 - 40 amp Electronic speed control</li><li>1 - 3-cell 1500mAH LiPo Battery or 4-cell 1500mAH Battery</li><li>1 - LiPo Battery Charger</li><li>1 - 4 channel or larger receiver</li><li>1- 4 channel or larger transmitter</li><li>Pushrod wires</li><li>Hot glue sticks</li><li>Hot Glue gun</li><li>10x10x8.5x420mm carbon fiber square tube</li><li>Thread (if using plywood tail)</li><li>Packaging tape or duct tape</li></ul>
<h4 id="optional-if-using-landing-gear">Optional, if using landing gear</h4>
<ul><li>2 mm axle</li><li>2 65mm or larger bush tires</li><li>Axle hub locks (can also use hot glue as a cheap alternative)</li></ul>
<h4 id="tools">Tools</h4>
<ul><li>X-acto Knife, breakaway knife, or straight razor blade</li><li>Metric tape measure</li><li>150mm metal ruler</li><li>Metal straight edge</li><li>Z-bend pliers</li><li>Small phillips screw driver</li><li>Needle nose pliers</li><li>Heavy duty wire cutters</li><li>Cutting mat (optional)</li><li>Hot glue gun</li><li>Needle (if using plywood tail)</li><li>Needle threader (optional if using plywood tail)</li><li>Metric allen (hex) key set</li></ul>
<h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
<p>This airplane has been designed to be easy to build, easy to fly, and as tolerant of crashes as possible. A hard crash is still likely to break something; however, the combination of 3D-printed parts, plywood, and foam construction means that most damaged components are inexpensive, quick, and easy to replace.</p>
<p>This guide is written with the assumption that you have no previous experience building or flying a fixed-wing UAS. If you prefer a faster build, however, feel free to simply follow the pictures—there are a lot of them! Most of the assembly is fairly intuitive, but some sections, such as wing construction and servo and pushrod installation, will require a little more attention to detail.</p>
<p>Most importantly, I hope you enjoy building this airplane and learning to fly it. Yes, you will crash, and yes, you will break things. That is all part of learning to fly. This airplane was specifically designed to tolerate many of the mistakes that beginning pilots make, while also making repairs simple when something does break.</p>
<p>The goal is not to build an airplane that never crashes. The goal is to build an airplane that lets you crash, repair, learn, and try again without becoming discouraged. With each flight, you will gain more confidence in your ability to both build and fly your own aircraft.</p>
<p>Good luck, and happy flying!</p>
<h2 id="where-to-start">Where to start?</h2>
<p>This airplane uses several 3D-printed parts, so getting those started first is a good way to begin the build. All of the printed components are made from either 64D TPU or PETG. Choose whatever colors you like—there is no need to match the colors shown in this guide.</p>
<p>The airplanes pictured in this guide were printed primarily on Bambu Lab P1S and P2S printers, with some smaller parts printed on an A1 Mini. In fact, every printed component except the landing gear will fit on an A1 Mini.</p>
<p>When purchasing TPU, make sure you select 64D TPU (64 durometer). The specific brand is not particularly important, but the durometer is. Softer TPU will produce parts that are much more flexible than intended.</p>
<p>A good place to start is the fuselage, since it has the longest print time. Getting it started first will give you plenty of time to work on the other parts of the airplane while it prints.</p>
<p>Once you have the parts printing, purchase any materials you might need then get started building your wing. The wing is large and very satisfying to have completed, but first, let’s talk about landing gear.</p>
<h4 id="landing-gear-or-no-landing-gear">Landing gear or no landing gear?</h4>
<p>Most people want landing gear on their first airplane. It looks cool, and taking off and landing on wheels sounds like fun. It is cool, and it is fun—but it also makes learning to fly more difficult.</p>
<p>For a new pilot, hand launching and belly landing a trainer is much easier. Landing gear adds weight and drag, which reduces flight time and efficiency. Small wheels also make taking off and landing on grass difficult, so landing gear generally works best from dirt, asphalt, or other hard surfaces. Unfortunately, hard surfaces are also much less forgiving when a landing does not go as planned. Grass is considerably gentler on a poorly landed airplane.</p>
<p>Landing gear is included in this design as an option; however, I recommend leaving it off your airplane until you are comfortable consistently landing in grass. Once you have developed good approach and landing skills, adding landing gear provides another fun challenge and allows you to practice conventional takeoffs and landings.</p>
